From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=EC=8B=AC=EC=83=81=EC=9B=90?= <dev.chamomile@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 15:08:10 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] version: binaries report <upstream>[+wick.<N>]

Why: wick releases carry upstream's version (v1.18.0), and wick's own fix
releases on that base are v1.18.0+wick.1, ...; but the binaries print
CARGO_PKG_VERSION, so a fix release cannot be told from the upstream
release it fixes, by `--version` or by `GET /version`.

What: the release label comes from wick-version (crates/version), which
reads patches/base and patches/version at build time and expands
`wick_version::version!()` to the literal `1.18.0` (fix number 0) or
`1.18.0+wick.N`: semver build metadata, so a fix release never sorts below
the upstream release, as a pre-release would. It replaces
env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION") in firecracker (`--version`, `--help`, the
start-up log line, `GET /version` and `vmm_version` in `GET /`), the
jailer, snapshot-editor, rebase-snap and seccompiler-bin. Format and
compatibility versions stay upstream's: the snapshot format and
`--snapshot-version`, the API specification's version, and
cpu-template-helper, whose version is written into CPU fingerprints that
`fingerprint compare` checks. The workspace lists crates/version as a
member. test_api_version compares Cargo.toml's version with the API's
without its build metadata.

Test: `cargo test -p wick-version` and
`cargo test -p firecracker --bin firecracker wick_version`; with
patches/version 1, `firecracker --version` prints
`Firecracker v1.18.0+wick.1` and `GET /version` returns
`{"firecracker_version":"1.18.0+wick.1"}`, while
`firecracker --snapshot-version` still prints `v12.0.0`.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
